            model:
              "On the one hand, there is evidence to agree that the failure of public health measures contributed to the high death toll. In 1348, medieval towns had no proper sewage systems, clean running water, or street cleaners. Filth, rotting meat offal, and human waste piled up in narrow unpaved streets, creating ideal breeding grounds for rats and fleas. Although some towns tried to protect themselves—for example, Gloucester attempted to quarantine itself by shutting its town gates to people from infected Bristol—the regulations were poorly enforced, and rural traders easily bypassed the gates. In London, authorities were completely overwhelmed and only organized emergency burial trenches after thousands were already dead. Therefore, public health measures failed to prevent mass deaths because town councils lacked the power, money, and organization to enforce effective quarantine or clean the streets.<br><br>On the other hand, the primary reason so many people died was that nobody understood the biological cause of the plague. In 1348, people had no knowledge of bacteria (Yersinia pestis) or flea bites from black rats. Instead, they believed the Black Death was caused by God’s wrath punishing human sins, or by miasma (poisonous air). Because of these false beliefs, civic and religious responses actually worsened the epidemic. For example, church authorities organized mass religious processions and prayer gatherings in crowded streets, which drastically accelerated the person-to-person spread of pneumonic plague. Similarly, carrying sweet-smelling posies and burning tar in the streets did nothing to stop fleas from biting people. Therefore, the overwhelming death toll was primarily caused by a total lack of medical understanding rather than public health failures, as authorities could never stop a disease they did not understand.<br><br>Furthermore, medieval medical treatments were completely ineffective and often hastened patients' deaths. Physicians followed Galen's Four Humours, using bloodletting with leeches and violent purging, which severely weakened patients' immune systems right when their bodies needed strength to fight the infection. Apothecaries prescribed costly herbal remedies that had no effect on bacteria, while barbers lanced swollen buboes with dirty knives, exposing victims to fatal blood poisoning. Because doctors did not possess modern antibiotics like streptomycin, anyone who caught pneumonic plague faced a 100% death rate. Therefore, high mortality was caused by the total failure of medical cures, as doctors were completely powerless against the plague bacteria.<br><br>In conclusion, I agree only to a limited extent that people died because of failed public health measures. While medieval town councils were disorganized and failed to enforce effective quarantine, even the cleanest medieval town would still have been devastated by the plague. The primary reason for the catastrophic mortality (which killed around a third of England's population) was the complete absence of scientific knowledge regarding germs and rat fleas. Therefore, while public health measures were weak, it was the fundamental lack of medical understanding that made mass death completely unavoidable.",
