          content:
            '<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> Throughout the eighteenth century, smallpox (*Variola major*) was the most terrifying epidemic killer in Britain. An airborne viral infection that spread effortlessly through crowded tenements, smallpox killed an estimated 40,000 people in Britain every year, claiming the lives of one in three newborn infants. Victims suffered agonizing fevers, severe vomiting, and erupted into thousands of fluid-filled pustules that fused together across the body. Those who survived were often left blind, disfigured, or permanently scarred with grotesque pitted pockmarks.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> The only known protection was *inoculation* (or variolation), brought to Britain from Turkey in 1721 by aristocrat Lady Mary Wortley Montagu. Inoculation involved using a lancet to scrape live, infectious smallpox pus from a sick patient’s pustule directly into a cut on a healthy person’s arm. While patients frequently survived with a milder infection and gained lifelong immunity, the procedure was fraught with mortal peril: roughly 2% to 3% of inoculated patients died from full-blown smallpox, and inoculated individuals became highly contagious vectors who ignited fresh community epidemics. Furthermore, commercial inoculators like the Sutton and Dimsdale families charged extortionate fees (up to 20 guineas), making prevention a luxury monopoly reserved exclusively for the wealthy.',
