              content:
                "In order to ascertain whether the cowpox was a certain preservative against smallpox, I selected a healthy boy, about eight years old, named James Phipps. The matter was taken from a sore on the hand of a dairymaid, Sarah Nelmes, who was infected by her master's cows, and it was inserted on the 14th of May, 1796, into the arm of the boy by means of two superficial incisions... In order to satisfy myself whether the boy was secure against smallpox contagion, on the 1st of July following, I inoculated him with variolous matter taken immediately from a pustule of smallpox. No disease followed... What renders the cowpox so peculiarly eligible is that it produces no pustules of a pestilential nature on the body, nor can it infect by effluvia.",
