          content:
            '<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> Over the following decades, the British government steadily tightened state enforcement: the 1852 Compulsory Vaccination Act made infant vaccination legally mandatory within three months of birth, and the 1867 Vaccination Act introduced strict financial fines and imprisonment for non-compliant parents. In response, working-class communities formed the Anti-Vaccination League, marching in mass demonstrations in Leicester and London against what they denounced as medical tyranny and infringement on bodily autonomy.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> Despite civil liberties protests, compulsory vaccination caused national smallpox deaths to plummet from over 40,000 per decade to virtually zero by the end of the century. However, historians emphasize that Jenner’s breakthrough was an extraordinary "one-off" empirical anomaly. Because Jenner lived six decades before Louis Pasteur’s Germ Theory (1861), he had no comprehension of viruses or immune antibodies. Unable to explain the biological mechanism of immunity, Jenner could not adapt his technique to prevent any other infectious disease, leaving cholera, typhoid, and tuberculosis to rampage across Victorian Britain untouched.',
