          content:
            '<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> In the first half of the nineteenth century, British hospitals were squalid, dangerous institutions. Rather than centers of cure, municipal voluntary hospitals and poor law infirmaries were regarded by the working classes as "death houses" of last resort. Wards were cramped, airless, and dark; beds were pushed tightly together with three or four patients sharing a single straw mattress contaminated with blood, feces, and vomit (<span class="archival-meta-tag">Source A</span>). Surgeons operated in filthy, blood-stiffened frock coats without washing hands or sterilizing equipment, moving directly from post-mortem autopsies to open surgical wounds. Consequently, patients who survived operations almost universally died from post-operative infections known collectively as "hospitalism": lethal hospital gangrene, erysipelas, septicaemia, and pyaemia.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> The standard of nursing was equally disgraceful. Nurses were completely untrained, illiterate, poorly paid, and widely regarded as drunken, disreputable domestic servants who neglected vulnerable patients—immortalized in Charles Dickens’ satirical caricature of Betsy Prig. Middle-class women refused to enter the trade, leaving ward care to uneducated drudges who lacked any basic understanding of hygiene, dressing wounds, or infection control. In 1854, Britain entered the Crimean War against Russia. Sensational eyewitness dispatches published in *The Times* by William Howard Russell ignited public fury in London: British soldiers were dying in thousands not on the battlefield, but from rampant cholera, dysentery, and frostbite inside military hospitals that lacked bandages, food, soap, or clean water.',
