          content:
            '<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> In response to the Crimean crisis, Secretary at War Sidney Herbert commissioned Florence Nightingale—an educated, upper-class woman who had studied nursing in Germany—to lead an official mission of thirty-eight volunteer nurses to the British army base hospital at Scutari near Constantinople in November 1854. What Nightingale discovered was an administrative nightmare: the massive Barrack Hospital was constructed directly over a gigantic, blocked cesspool; raw sewage backed up onto the floor; drinking water was contaminated; and hundreds of soldiers lay in lice-infested uniforms on bare flagstones alongside rotting carcasses of dead horses.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Nightingale encountered fierce hostility from military doctors, who resented female civilian interference in army discipline. Undaunted, Nightingale used her personal fortune and Times relief funds to purchase clean shirts, bandages, and scrubbing brushes. She organized military-style sanitary discipline: cleaning wards with boiling water, operating kitchens to serve nourishing broths, washing soldiers’ clothes in separate laundry facilities, and opening windows for fresh air (<span class="archival-meta-tag">Source B</span>). In March 1855, after Nightingale lobbied Parliament, a government Sanitary Commission arrived in Scutari and flushed the underground sewers. Within months, the hospital mortality rate plummeted spectacularly from over 42% to just 2%.',
