                'I applied to the War Office and to the Nightingale Fund to be sent out as a nurse to the Crimea, but was repeatedly rebuffed... Did these ladies shrink from accepting my aid because by blood and complexion I was of a darker caste? I resolved that if the authorities would not send me, I would go at my own expense. At Spring Hill, two miles from Sevastopol, I built the British Hotel. I had medicines and bandages continually ready. Many a time I attended poor fellows right upon the battlefield under fire, bandaging crushed limbs, staunching bleeding wounds, and administering warm broths and my own herbal cordials to soldiers dying of dysentery and cholera.',
              question_badge: 'INDIVIDUAL ENTERPRISE & RACIAL RESILIENCE',
              question:
                "Explain how Mary Seacole's work in the Crimea differed from official government military medical care.",
              model_answer:
                "Seacole's work was entirely independent and self-funded after the War Office rejected her due to racial prejudice. Rather than managing distant base hospitals behind the lines, Seacole operated directly near the front lines, personally bandaging wounded soldiers on the battlefield and treating dysentery with her own Jamaican herbal remedies.",
