              content:
                'The very first requirement in a hospital is that it should do the sick no harm. Yet in our existing crowded, poorly-built infirmaries, diseases are generated which kill more patients than their original ailments. The only true architectural design is the Pavilion Plan. Wards must be separate detached blocks, connected only by an open corridor, with tall opposing windows along both walls to allow a continuous draught of fresh external air. Ceilings must be high, floors made of polished impermeable wood, walls painted with non-porous plaster that can be washed down with carbolic, and beds placed at least eight feet apart to prevent the accumulation of foul hospital miasma.',
              question_badge: 'HOSPITAL ARCHITECTURE & INFECTION CONTROL',
