          content:
            '<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> In the early decades of the nineteenth century, surgery was an ordeal of unimaginable agony. Surgeons faced three insurmountable physiological barriers: *Pain*, *Infection*, and *Bleeding*. Because there were no effective anaesthetics, operations were confined to emergency amputations, superficial tumor excisions, and lithotomies (bladder stone cuttings). Patients had to be dragged into operating theatres and physically pinned to wooden tables by burly hospital porters while screaming in terror. Many patients died on the table from sheer cardiovascular shock before the knife had completed its work.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Under these terrifying conditions, surgical excellence was judged solely by speed. The premier surgeon of the era, London’s Robert Liston, was celebrated for amputating an adult thigh in under thirty seconds. Liston operated in a dark frock coat stiffened with dried blood and rotting pus from past surgeries, holding his bone saw between his teeth to free his hands. Speed was the only mercy a surgeon could offer, but speed made delicate, precise operations on the abdomen, chest, or brain completely impossible.',
