            '<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Early chemical experiments with pain relief produced mixed results: laughing gas (nitrous oxide) was weak, while ether (first demonstrated by American dentist William Morton in 1846) irritated patients’ lungs, induced violent vomiting, and created an explosive fire hazard near gaslights. In November 1847, Dr James Young Simpson, Professor of Midwifery at Edinburgh University, met with colleagues Dr Keith and Dr Duncan at his home at 52 Queen Street to sample vapors. After inhaling *chloroform*, the doctors felt dizzy and exhilarated before tumbling unconscious beneath the dining table (<span class="archival-meta-tag">Source A</span>). Recognizing that chloroform was fast-acting, potent, and non-flammable, Simpson immediately began administering it to relieve the agony of childbirth and surgical operations.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Chloroform triggered intense opposition from religious and medical quarters. Clergymen argued that God had cursed Eve in Genesis to bring forth children in pain, claiming anaesthesia was a sinful evasion of divine will. Doctors warned of lethal toxicity: in 1848, fifteen-year-old Hannah Greener died from ventricular fibrillation during a minor toenail operation after receiving an uncontrolled chloroform dose. Opposition finally collapsed in 1853, when Dr John Snow developed a precision chloroform inhaler mask and administered it to Queen Victoria during the birth of Prince Leopold, winning royal endorsement for the new anaesthetic.',
