              citation:
                "Godfrey Hounsfield, 'Computed Medical Imaging', Nobel Lecture in Physiology or Medicine, Stockholm, 1979.",
              content:
                'Conventional X-ray techniques suffer from two fundamental limitations. First, it is impossible to separate the superimposed shadow of bones from soft tissue organs lying behind or in front of them; the image is a flattened two-dimensional projection of a three-dimensional body. Second, conventional radiographs are entirely incapable of distinguishing between soft tissues whose chemical densities differ by less than ten per cent... In computed tomography, an X-ray source rotates 360 degrees around the patient, taking hundreds of thousands of readings through narrow planar slices. A digital computer processes these attenuation values, calculating absolute tissue densities and displaying an accurate transverse picture of soft tissues accurate to within one half of one per cent.',
              question_badge: 'DIAGNOSTIC RESOLUTION & COMPUTER COMPUTATION',
              question:
                'Explain the diagnostic advantages of the CT scanner over traditional X-rays according to Source D.',
              model_answer:
                'According to Source D, CT scanners overcome the two major flaws of conventional X-rays: they eliminate overlapping 2D shadows by producing cross-sectional 3D slices, and their digital computer processing is sensitive enough to differentiate soft tissues (such as healthy brain tissue versus tumours or blood clots) whose densities differ by as little as 0.5%.',
