              content:
                'The great infectious killers of the past—cholera, typhus, smallpox, and tuberculosis—have been conquered through clean water, improved housing, vaccination, and antibiotics. Today, the chief causes of premature death in Britain are non-communicable lifestyle conditions: coronary heart disease, stroke, and cancers of the lung and bowel... Modern medicine possesses extraordinary technological wizardry: intensive care units, coronary bypass grafts, kidney dialysis, and joint replacements. Yet these high-tech interventions are vastly expensive and merely salvage patients after organs are irreparably damaged. The future of healthcare lies not in hospital cure, but in individual prevention: reducing cigarette smoking, moderating alcohol consumption, adopting balanced diets low in animal fat and salt, and taking regular physical exercise.',
