          content:
            '<span class="para-ref">[5.1]</span> The foundation of the NHS coincided with an unprecedented explosion in high-tech treatments and complex surgery. In 1962, British orthopedic surgeon Sir John Charnley perfected low-friction total hip replacement at Wrightington Hospital using high-density polyethylene and bone cement, restoring mobility to hundreds of thousands of arthritic patients. The NHS rapidly expanded to deliver kidney dialysis machines, cardiac bypass surgery, intensive neonatal life support, and complex organ transplants.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[5.2]</span> Paradoxically, the clinical success of the NHS in keeping an ageing population alive longer created an escalating financial crisis. When the service opened, pent-up demand resulted in 8.5 million pairs of free spectacles and millions of dentures dispensed within months, wildly exceeding budgetary forecasts. By 1951, the government was forced to introduce prescription and dental charges, prompting Bevan’s Cabinet resignation. In the modern era, the exponential cost of new biological therapies, robotic surgery, and round-the-clock emergency care continues to challenge Bevan’s founding vision of universal, tax-funded healthcare.',
          theme: 'High-Tech Care, Surgical Milestones & Escalating Costs (1950s–Present)',
