            '<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> During the First World War, Scottish bacteriologist Alexander Fleming witnessed thousands of soldiers die from wound sepsis in French military hospitals. Fleming recognized that standard chemical antiseptics like carbolic acid did more harm than good in deep lacerations, because chemicals destroyed the patient’s protective white blood cells faster than they eliminated bacteria. Fleming dedicated his post-war career at St Mary’s Hospital, London, to discovering an antibacterial agent that attacked microbes without poisoning human tissue.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> In late August 1928, Fleming left culture plates of *Staphylococcus* bacteria stacked in his Paddington laboratory before departing on holiday. Fungal spores from a mycologist’s laboratory below drifted through the open window, landing on an uncovered dish. Returning on 3 September 1928, Fleming examined the plate (<span class="archival-meta-tag">Source A</span>) and noticed a clear, bacteria-free halo around a blob of blue-green mould where colonies of golden bacteria had dissolved. Fleming identified the mould as *Penicillium notatum*, naming its secretion **Penicillin**. He published his findings in 1929; however, lacking biochemical expertise to purify the unstable juice, he abandoned clinical trials.',
