              content:
                'A statistical investigation was carried out in twenty London hospitals to determine whether patients suffering from cancer of the lung differed in their smoking habits from patients suffering from other diseases... Among 649 men with carcinoma of the lung, only two (0.3%) were non-smokers, compared with twenty-seven (4.2%) in the control group. Among heavy smokers (consuming 25 or more cigarettes a day), the ratio was even more striking: 26.0% of the lung cancer patients were heavy smokers compared with only 13.5% of the controls... The association between smoking and carcinoma of the lung is not due to chance. It is concluded that smoking is a factor, and an important factor, in the production of carcinoma of the lung.',
