              image_url: '/images/trench_foot_clinical.jpg',
              caption:
                'RAMC clinical photograph documenting severe bilateral trench foot in a British infantryman (c.1915). Prolonged immersion in freezing, waterlogged mud constricted blood vessels, resulting in tissue death, blistering, and wet gangrene.',
              citation: 'The Wellcome Collection, London (Wellcome Images / Public Domain).',
              question_badge: 'NON-COMBAT PATHOLOGY: TRENCH FOOT & GANGRENE',
              question_type: 'Source Utility (Paper 1 Section A Q2a)',
              question:
                'Study Source A. How useful is Source A for an enquiry into the nature and physical severity of trench foot among British soldiers on the Western Front?',
              model_answer:
                '<p style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source A is exceptionally useful because it provides stark, clinical visual evidence of the physical destruction caused by trench foot (trench frostbite). The medical photograph reveals swollen, blackened, and blistered feet where capillary blood circulation has ceased, resulting in necrosis and wet gangrene across the toes. This directly reflects historical reality: soldiers standing for consecutive days in freezing, stagnant mud and waterlogged trenches suffered constricted blood vessels; prolonged damp caused tissues to die, which frequently turned gangrenous when bacteria infected the dead flesh. If left untreated, amputation was the only surgical intervention to prevent lethal sepsis. In the winter of 1914–1915 alone, over 20,000 British soldiers were treated for trench foot, threatening British combat strength.</p><p style="margin: 0;"><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The provenance enhances its utility: as an official clinical medical photograph recorded by the Royal Army Medical Corps for clinical reference (nature and origin), it captures the exact diagnostic pathology objectively without propagandistic distortion. However, its utility is limited to the physical, biological symptoms of the disease in an advanced individual case; it does not reveal the operational preventative measures later enforced by the British Army—such as the mandatory application of whale oil grease, daily buddy-system foot inspections, changing dry woollen socks twice daily, or the construction of raised duckboard sumps—which successfully reduced trench foot admissions by over 80% by 1917.</p>',
