              citation: 'Official Medical History of the Great War (HMSO / Public Domain).',
              content:
                'In August and September 1914, during the retreat from Mons, tetanus was a dreadful scourge. Over 32 out of every 1,000 wounded men developed tetanus, with agonizing muscular spasms (lockjaw) and an almost 80% fatality rate. The cause was direct contamination by the manured soil of northern France.\nIn October 1914, the routine preventative administration of antitetanic serum was made compulsory at every Regimental Aid Post. Stretcher bearers and medical officers were ordered to inject 500 units of serum immediately into every casualty before evacuation rearward. By 1917, the incidence of tetanus had fallen to less than 1.4 per 1,000 casualties. This single preventative measure saved tens of thousands of lives.',
