          model_answer:
            '<p style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source A is exceptionally useful because it directly illustrates the extreme physical agony and logistical impediments of frontline casualty evacuation in the Flanders theatre. The photograph shows a team of six British stretcher bearers struggling shoulder-high to carry a wounded man across a devastated landscape of cratered liquid mud and splintered tree trunks. This directly corroborates historical knowledge: during the Third Battle of Ypres (Passchendaele) in autumn 1917, relentless shellfire destroyed all canal drainage systems, turning the low-lying clay into deep morasses. While a standard stretcher team comprised two to four bearers, the immense suction of the mud and the risk of the wounded soldier sliding off or drowning meant six men were needed per stretcher, making evacuation runs of even two miles take up to eight hours and rapidly exhausting medical personnel.</p><p style="margin: 0;"><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The provenance strongly supports its utility: as an authentic photograph taken by an official British war photographer on the active battlefield during the Passchendaele offensive (nature and origin), it captures genuine combat conditions without theatrical staging. However, its utility is focused on the extreme environmental conditions of the Ypres Salient; it does not illustrate evacuation in sectors with firmer ground (such as the chalk terrain of Arras or the rolling hills of the Somme in dry weather), nor does it reveal subsequent stages of the evacuation chain, such as motorized ambulance transport, surgical triage at Casualty Clearing Stations, or treatment on hospital trains.</p>',
