          citation: "The War the Infantry Knew 1914–1919 (Jane's Publishing / Public Domain).",
          content:
            'The Regimental Aid Post was set up in a collapsed German dugout 200 yards behind the frontline trench. Throughout the bombardment, casualties poured in in an unending stream. My duty as Battalion Medical Officer is not to perform operations, but to execute rapid triage: stop severe arterial haemorrhage with tourniquets, dress wounds with sterile gauze, inject antitetanic serum, administer morphine to ease agony, and label each man with a colored tag.\nThe slightly wounded are bandaged and sent back to the firing line; men with severe shrapnel lacerations or fractured limbs are tagged for immediate stretcher transport to the Advanced Dressing Station; for the hopeless, dying cases with massive brain trauma, we can only provide morphine and a quiet corner to die.',
