          model_answer:
            '<p style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source A is highly useful because it provides rare, authentic photographic documentation of the operational environment inside a Casualty Clearing Station (CCS) operating theatre during a major offensive. The photograph shows multiple surgical teams working simultaneously on adjacent operating tables in a large tented complex illuminated by electric lamps, surrounded by sterilized bowls, surgical instruments, and trained nurses. This directly corroborates historical reality: between 1914 and 1917, Casualty Clearing Stations transformed from minor dressing stations into advanced surgical hospitals located 10 to 12 miles behind the front line. Surgeons performed critical life-saving operations—such as laparotomies for abdominal wounds, deep debridement, and amputations—within the critical \'golden hours\' before bacterial gangrene could take hold, saving tens of thousands of soldiers who would have died during lengthy transit to coastal base hospitals.</p><p style="margin: 0;"><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The provenance enhances its utility: as an official photograph taken by an accredited military photographer for British official records in 1917 (nature and origin), it captures the true scale and professional organization of military surgery under wartime pressure. However, the source presents a staged, orderly scene; it does not convey the overwhelming chaos during mass-casualty barrages, the agonizing backlog of hundreds of stretchers lying outside in freezing rain, the extreme exhaustion of surgeons operating for 36 hours without break, or the high postoperative mortality from surgical shock and anaerobic gas infection.</p>',
