              citation:
                "Harvey Cushing, 'From a Surgeon's Journal: 1915–1918' (Little, Brown and Company / Public Domain).",
              content:
                'At Casualty Clearing Station 46 near Ypres, the influx of head injuries during the Passchendaele offensive is continuous. Hitherto, penetrating wounds of the skull carried a mortality of over 50 to 60 per cent, largely because rough probing tore healthy brain tissue and introduced sepsis.\nWe have instituted a new operating technique: using local novocaine anaesthesia rather than general ether to prevent cerebral venous congestion, we excise the scalp wound, elevate fractured skull fragments with trephines, and use a small rubber catheter attached to a suction bulb to extract macerated brain tissue, bone chips, and shrapnel fragments. A small electromagnet is then introduced to draw out deep steel splinters. In our last series of 219 consecutive cranial operations, mortality dropped to 28.8 per cent.',
