          citation: 'The British Journal of Surgery / Wellcome Collection (Public Domain).',
          content:
            "The men who came to us from the front had faces shattered by bullet and shell fragments: noses blown away, jaws pulverized, eyelids burned off by chemical gas. In civilian life, such men were objects of horror who shrank from public view, avoiding their own families.\nAt the Queen's Hospital in Sidcup, opened in 1917, we established an institution designed entirely for their rehabilitation. We developed the 'tubed pedicle' graft: a flap of skin was cut from the chest or neck, rolled into a cylinder with the raw surface inside, and stitched together. This ensured the pedicle retained its own living blood supply while one end was swung up and attached to the face. Once new blood vessels had grown into the facial tissues after three weeks, the other end was severed and shaped into a new nose, jaw, or eyelid. Over 11,000 surgical procedures were carried out, restoring not merely faces, but human dignity.",
